  "_style": "Address the user with informal „du“ (dech, der, däin/deng/däi) at all times; address the couple together with informal plural „dir“ (iech, äre/är/äert) or „dir zwee“, never the formal „Dir“. The partner is always „däi Schatz“ (de Schatz works for any gender), or rephrase neutrally with the name placeholder %@, „dir zwee“ or „déi aner Persoun“; never Frënd/Frëndin, Mann/Fra, and avoid gendered pronouns (hien/si) and gendered forms for the user or partner by rephrasing (e.g. „Bass du sécher?“ is fine, but no „Partnerin“). A partner's name placeholder (%@) is never declined or given a genitive -s (not „%@s Bréif“ but „de Bréif vun %@“ / „Bréif vun %@“); rephrase instead. Use standard Luxembourgish spelling (ZLS 2019) and apply the n-rule (Eifeler Regel) consistently: final -n drops before consonants other than n, d, t, z, h and vowels (e.g. „Zesumme genetzt“, „mat dengem Schatz“). Quote marks: German-style „ “ outer, ‚ ‘ inner; ellipsis as the single character …; no space before ? ! : ; ; keep „ · “ separators, emoji, \\n and every placeholder (%@, %lld, %1$@) exactly. Numbers: decimal comma, thin space or dot for thousands (1.000), dates as „14. November 2026“ / „14.11.2026“, 24-hour time „18:30“. Capitalize all nouns as Luxembourgish grammar requires; otherwise titles, tabs and headers are sentence case (only the first word and nouns capitalized, e.g. „Fro vum Dag“). Buttons and actions are short infinitives without „wgl.“ (Späicheren, Schécken, Bréif versigelen, Nach eng Kéier). Write „KI“ for AI, keep „Coupley“, „Premium“ and „CoupleSync“ untranslated. Latin script only. Plural categories: \"one\" and \"other\" („%lld Dag“ / „%lld Deeg“). Tone: warm, light and natural, like a friend writing — never cheesy, never word-for-word from German.",
